Private Allocate/Deallocate pool regressions
==========================================

Build test-exec-privatepool or test-exec-privatepool-quick. Both belong to
the Exec test component and are available on every AROS target; these tests
do not use Amiga hardware. Run exec/privatepool/privatepool on the target.
Results and failures are printed to the Shell and the debug/serial output.
A native m68k run uses the real eight-byte MemChunk layout.

The tests exercise UFO's 128140-byte private pool and all terminal remainder
sizes below MEMCHUNK_TOTAL. They exhaust the usable space, free blocks in
every order, and verify original free counts, coalescing, returned addresses,
and guards immediately outside the declared pool. Empty headers are populated
through Deallocate with every terminal remainder and unaligned lower bounds.
Tests also cover informational lower bounds, sub-chunk regions, and frees
that explicitly include an already-counted orphan tail.

A mixed-size stress test checks live data and free-list invariants after
every operation. On AROS, four processes also share one pool through the
public Allocate/Deallocate API; each verifies its blocks before freeing
them. This detects task switches during a free-list update. Non-SMP tests
also verify that calls preserve the caller's Forbid nesting.

On a 64-bit host, also run:

    python3 host_test.py

or select test-exec-privatepool-host / test-exec-privatepool-host-quick.
The script compiles the production validation, stdAlloc, stdDealloc, NOMMU
functions and public Allocate/Deallocate wrapper bodies using a small host
ABI adapter.
It does not duplicate the allocation algorithm. AddressSanitizer and
UndefinedBehaviorSanitizer check 16- and 32-byte alignments, with and without
the SMP path. The SMP cases run concurrent allocations/deallocations using
host atomic locks. Additional checks cover reverse allocation, requests
larger than 4 GiB delegated to managed allocators, oversized requests/frees,
and rejection of corrupt aligned pools. System-memory checks cover largest
allocatable size and AllocAbs splitting of uneven terminal chunks. Public
free bounds must produce the same alert for one- and two-byte overruns.
Scheduling checks keep a pending reschedule deferred across Allocate and
Deallocate, preserve existing nesting, and allow NULL-SysBase early boot.
These are allocator tests, not a boot of a complete 64-bit AROS kernel.

To exercise eight-byte chunks on a host, use WASI SDK and Node.js:

    python3 host_test.py --wasi-sdk /path/to/wasi-sdk

The optional WASI runs compile the same production code with actual 32-bit
pointers and sizes, so sizeof(struct MemChunk) is eight, as on m68k. Both
non-SMP and SMP paths run, but WASI is single-threaded and has no sanitizer;
threaded SMP and ASan/UBSan checks remain in the 64-bit runs. Never set an
eight-byte alignment on a 64-bit MemChunk: its header is sixteen bytes.
The normal/quick host make targets include WASI when WASI_SDK_PATH is set.

For a before/after check, pass an earlier production memory.c with --source
and --expect-failure. Its memory_nommu.c, allocate.c and deallocate.c must
be beside it. A failing original allocator is expected to return
status 20 from its test binary. Sanitizer failures are never a passing result
for the corrected allocator.
